why none of them is neutral."}, {"id": "2329040d-96c2-48eb-b81d-1ce67cb5af25", "type": "subtopic", "title": "World-literature debates", "children": [{"id": "45d2a02a-0bcf-44d1-8559-82662a34e8bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Goethe and Weltliteratur"}, {"id": "26ebbdb0-ec7a-47c5-bed4-334bda52bf3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Damrosch and circulation"}, {"id": "17e559d8-fade-4fee-b02c-48f1d0fbb156", "type": "concept", "title": "Moretti and distant reading"}, {"id": "1dffd231-3ada-4aff-ad07-7659d2437716", "type": "concept", "title": "Casanova and literary world-systems"}, {"id": "7dff7502-02ef-406f-8340-ddc663b3c0f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Apter and critiques of untranslatability"}], "description": "Students learn that world literature can mean a canon, a reading method, a translation market, or a system of circulation."}, {"id": "b71838bf-fdc2-44de-b2e1-fe7fd7fe2349", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Responsible comparison", "children": [{"id": "2086bad4-badb-4882-b854-866db9262f67", "type": "concept", "title": "close reading before comparison"}, {"id": "edaff832-20ac-4f1d-a2d5-f90d8ac01c53", "type": "concept", "title": "historical specificity"}, {"id": "45c3903b-9432-47ce-b38d-1fab57308d6b", "type": "concept", "title": "formal comparison"}, {"id": "31d1c6e7-4e3f-4bb7-ae68-032d7e6d49b0", "type": "concept", "title": "thematic comparison"}, {"id": "7eb66d02-0b4f-487d-b72a-5b0888176906", "type": "concept", "title": "reception comparison"}, {"id": "303ec80c-ff16-4e2f-8f9c-46bd4f7df403", "type": "concept", "title": "limits of evidence"}], "description": "The goal is to compare across texts without flattening difference or using one culture as the silent norm."}]}, {"id": "f36983d2-1ba0-4b3c-ba42-6291e0896386", "type": "topic", "title": "Translation, Multilingualism, and Untranslatability", "description": "Translation as method, mediation, power, language texture, and interpretive problem.", "children": [{"id": "95488642-04de-4e45-a662-939d935ff609", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation as interpretation", "children": [{"id": "51efa882-226d-4676-bf75-caef4ab24717", "type": "concept", "title": "translator visibility"}, {"id": "1cc19fdd-96ef-4fd8-8a61-7e255d3f8267", "type": "concept", "title": "literal versus literary translation"}, {"id": "d9458a42-dc51-4918-bc3b-f77b0a4e41ee", "type": "concept", "title": "paratexts"}, {"id": "641f2865-a04d-41a7-816f-42b70383b015", "type": "concept", "title": "glossaries"}, {"id": "f0973b81-4e56-4835-b7e5-4320c9ea2d58", "type": "concept", "title": "domestication and foreignization"}, {"id": "a76d182e-ac4e-4dc2-b002-142b4fcca8c0", "type": "concept", "title": "relay translation"}], "description": "Translation is not a transparent transfer of meaning; it is a set of choices about sound, register, form, audience, and power."}, {"id": "7801a380-80d0-4bca-9f60-a6300af70bdd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Multilingual literary worlds", "children": [{"id": "2d47cf5c-59a4-47c5-8708-43bae5ae3d1f", "type": "concept", "title": "code-switching"}, {"id": "ba82cee3-98ea-4044-af17-80300bb3e9ba", "type": "concept", "title": "diglossia"}, {"id": "a9375233-f7af-4f3e-9b98-e1db1befc155", "type": "concept", "title": "Creole and vernacular writing"}, {"id": "0c558f80-c056-42e8-8846-955d12760f3e", "type": "concept", "title": "self-translation"}, {"id": "da105048-ae84-450d-b799-4189b3c6be88", "type": "concept", "title": "colonial education"}, {"id": "435e7974-83f1-4759-aa25-b4b7b937b45a", "type": "concept", "title": "language hierarchy"}], "description": "World literature often emerges from contact zones where languages overlap, compete, and transform each other."}, {"id": "6c052556-11f0-4105-9d73-5c9d49c1160b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Untranslatability and method", "children": [{"id": "984da2d0-40d2-471f-87b3-0e1081ce587b", "type": "concept", "title": "idiom"}, {"id": "d6e2dbd8-4c41-4e57-bce4-5a1d76598a24", "type": "concept", "title": "wordplay"}, {"id": "dad18f81-df72-4bec-a09d-c85515dd8b03", "type": "concept", "title": "sacred language"}, {"id": "890c784c-f28d-4a70-a44f-fe0a25cf4e5f", "type": "concept", "title": "oral performance"}, {"id": "51c2d12e-3d1d-4a6f-9809-538943cd5473", "type": "concept", "title": "cultural reference"}, {"id": "dd692860-b91c-4785-b301-e636e099b887", "type": "concept", "title": "sound pattern"}, {"id": "44002117-c135-4f93-84f8-8db935752dba", "type": "concept", "title": "translator notes"}], "description": "Students learn to notice what resists translation and how that resistance can become an interpretive clue."}]}, {"id": "ebd57f50-fa11-452c-ae34-46fc1f0390d5", "type": "topic", "title": "Ancient and Classical World Literatures", "description": "Early epics, lyric, drama, wisdom literature, narrative frames, and foundational literary forms across regions.", "children": [{"id": "b348d234-01bc-4840-aefb-a47b35403998", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Epic and heroic narrative", "children": [{"id": "297bd1bc-af89-424a-b940-0d0090983f81", "type": "concept", "title": "Gilgamesh"}, {"id": "c5018bf9-37a1-4b2c-9b88-b5c4e8962405", "type": "concept", "title": "Homeric epic"}, {"id": "6d6bcd8b-9b13-4c4a-b378-9d630434150c", "type": "concept", "title": "Mahabharata and Ramayana"}, {"id": "a894302d-d183-4a5c-a900-f4475a699744", "type": "concept", "title": "classical Chinese narrative"}, {"id": "bbc6c3d2-a0c7-4d15-bb3b-c73df70033ba", "type": "concept", "title": "oral-formulaic composition"}, {"id": "3437f3da-f83c-4df5-b7d8-a15cc77644b8", "type": "concept", "title": "journey and return"}], "description": "Epic traditions help students see how societies imagine heroism, violence, memory, cosmology, and political order."}, {"id": "3f217795-d71f-4418-bd0d-450d9e5943e6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lyric, drama, and performance", "children": [{"id": "1c3b1a25-52a5-4bf7-93ea-6eab7d8bd034", "type": "concept", "title": "Greek tragedy and comedy"}, {"id": "6c37da2a-191e-4ad0-909f-d17017e00526", "type": "concept", "title": "Sanskrit drama"}, {"id": "2de95e2b-366a-4f90