 motifs"}], "description": "Frame narratives show how stories move across languages, religions, empires, and trade routes."}, {"id": "c0f921bc-a552-422d-8b5d-90ac1732c2bb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Travel, pilgrimage, and encounter", "children": [{"id": "06c6631b-8373-43b8-a8bc-bd36c15751e3", "type": "concept", "title": "pilgrimage"}, {"id": "31e0dc46-4c94-4db8-9d62-cb5af583d192", "type": "concept", "title": "travel writing"}, {"id": "4646857c-62ca-4dc6-85ea-b016f08632a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Silk Road"}, {"id": "96dbec95-365e-4576-8ee6-89e71e19fc5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian Ocean"}, {"id": "2d4aebf0-b207-4401-bcd8-b4d90da56b0a", "type": "concept", "title": "crusade and contact"}, {"id": "734e6cce-2ba3-41d7-b483-0ac36943a556", "type": "concept", "title": "marvels and ethnography"}], "description": "Medieval world literature records movement, curiosity, fear, commerce, mission, and cross-cultural imagination."}]}, {"id": "53db9f12-4755-47c7-ab4e-a4990f459288", "type": "topic", "title": "Early Modern Encounters, Empire, and Print", "description": "Encounter, conquest, print, humanism, performance, translation, and expanding literary worlds.", "children": [{"id": "374328ec-9673-49e9-b547-d2a11fe2b799", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Print and literary publics", "children": [{"id": "81d06a5f-364b-4dda-a070-175dd14628e6", "type": "concept", "title": "printing press"}, {"id": "d6aee07a-a621-434b-bb98-e716943ad406", "type": "concept", "title": "pamphlet culture"}, {"id": "5427d8e7-7ac9-4d1b-add5-54cfc1df9455", "type": "concept", "title": "book trade"}, {"id": "771c4ff6-6614-4854-a8df-c3bffabc744f", "type": "concept", "title": "censorship"}, {"id": "718db6fb-106e-4640-b36c-a43cff3290fd", "type": "concept", "title": "preface and dedication"}, {"id": "49a3e773-3774-46f6-b9db-fed403359654", "type": "concept", "title": "serial circulation"}], "description": "Print expanded readerships and altered authorship, authority, censorship, translation, and literary circulation."}, {"id": "6389e566-511f-4314-9916-835d964fe79e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Encounter and conquest", "children": [{"id": "cf50f488-41fd-485f-8729-f4ee789c4353", "type": "concept", "title": "travel narrative"}, {"id": "c222142a-a521-4825-b347-85963d40b631", "type": "concept", "title": "colonial archive"}, {"id": "6dc374c8-cb28-4e94-8537-99be6b4011ff", "type": "concept", "title": "missionary writing"}, {"id": "b7f9f7c4-288c-4ddd-a022-85aa58474ec6", "type": "concept", "title": "captivity narrative"}, {"id": "afcf820a-29e0-4536-aee3-1d087e210336", "type": "concept", "title": "racial classification"}, {"id": "d0647e67-6bd5-4aba-b621-3251cf2614cd", "type": "concept", "title": "indigenous testimony"}], "description": "Students examine how literature registered empire, conquest, travel, conversion, slavery, and the making of the New World."}, {"id": "2ff63aba-5d2f-4340-930a-418fe9a1e9f5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Drama, humanism, and global exchange", "children": [{"id": "563e7c76-2ce0-4bda-acca-b6ec40a80b3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Shakespeare and global adaptation"}, {"id": "5d498dd3-1abd-40e7-80b3-50bb4445934d", "type": "concept", "title": "Spanish Golden Age"}, {"id": "543ec624-a0e7-40a2-bf7f-86f740aa9e0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ming/Qing literary worlds"}, {"id": "96476af1-8a9c-4d42-9266-7b79f56f5fbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Ottoman/Persianate forms"}, {"id": "3bcd75a0-e64c-43bf-ae58-b4a63407b86b", "type": "concept", "title": "humanism"}, {"id": "9a522ece-99ba-421e-89d2-b6c0ad466c56", "type": "concept", "title": "translation and imitation"}], "description": "Early modern texts are read as local works within increasingly global systems of trade, empire, and performance."}]}, {"id": "300bc5aa-8bc2-441f-80d2-a9e0d5fa9ce4", "type": "topic", "title": "Enlightenment, Romanticism, and the World Republic of Letters", "description": "Cosmopolitanism, nationalism, revolution, empire, sentiment, and global aesthetic exchange.", "children": [{"id": "25a1d966-264d-4278-a9d0-97cf79478a2e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cosmopolitanism and empire", "children": [{"id": "c1a0e560-8096-4338-a128-8a2039c2d190", "type": "concept", "title": "cosmopolitanism"}, {"id": "757b5c8d-b6eb-4fa0-a792-e860a72e77d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism"}, {"id": "0251979e-9358-4f6b-9230-8b1a5431859f", "type": "concept", "title": "abolition"}, {"id": "58fa2e7c-93ae-4ec2-bb23-161fa5c51d7d", "type": "concept", "title": "rights discourse"}, {"id": "51da9cf2-1f19-4533-92c1-2569caec58d8", "type": "concept", "title": "encyclopedism"}, {"id": "7c5c9827-fc7e-45c7-ad0f-1844bea4b937", "type": "concept", "title": "travel and classification"}], "description": "Students study how ideals of reason, universality, rights, and progress coexisted with slavery, colonialism, and exclusion."}, {"id": "601a9bf2-e9a8-407b-9d7a-79406a5577b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Revolution and sentiment", "children": [{"id": "fbda3053-1f8d-499b-b087-38389e3b3d9b", "type": "concept", "title": "French and Haitian revolutions"}, {"id": "46bcdbac-65c6-469c-a275-5b66d11fe455", "type": "concept", "title": "sentimental novel"}, {"id": "1f769e2b-31e4-465f-871d-780285dfd6e4", "type": "concept", "title": "slave narrative"}, {"id": "d3f211b2-c4a1-4883-9fba-73f2e2151ef4", "type": "concept", "title": "letters"}, {"id": "38e4f8c5-84ec-4ea6-afd8-7318aa8aa031", "type": "concept", "title": "revolutionary poetry"}, {"id": "878c4b0f-f189-45ea-96cb-96024b6cc78c", "type": "concept", "title": "public sphere"}], "description": "Literature helped imagine revolution, sympathy, citizenship, national feeling, and critique of authority."}, {"id": "df704ec2-d571-4cd3-afad-5c911d465fc9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Romantic world-making", "children": [{"id": "c41c3bc7-b620-46b0-bcce-1c818c7600d4", "type": "concept", "title": "lyric subjectivity"}, {"id": "f079113a-e29a-4195-aec2-1f4e663da468", "type": "concept", "title": "folklore collection"}, {"id": "88ac1dd5-2b43-4d97-b4d5-1f8a6acc7e03", "type": "concept", "title": "sublime"}, {"id": "bb23d27a-747a-44f6-8871-48cc1e1b1105", "type": "concept", "title": "national epic"}, {"id": "a5e49d9a-6580-4b38-ae83-3808104a9bf1", "type": "concept", "title": "orientalist fantasy"}, {"id": "3b3b3c3d-8ce2-4cef-87cb-c67049093e23", "type": "concept", "title": "anti-imperial Romanticism"}], "description": "Romanticism is treated as a transnational movement shaped by nature, nation, folklore, translation, and empire."}]}, {"id": "8cad9230-d08c-40ed-9728-c3ed8e0d41d2", "type": "topic", "title": "The 19th-Century Global Novel and Realisms", "description": "The novel as a global form shaped by realism, colonial modernity, gender, class, nation, and capitalism.", "children": [{"id": "a85c567f-46d6-4f68-8b97-6e2401321aed", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The novel as global form", "children": [{"id": "bd5b6c34-63d1-4e8d-a844-53991567155c", "type": "concept", "title": "serial publication"}, {"id": "4aa98725-9022-4484-a5a6-5306d1b60d8b", "type": "concept", "title": "realism"}, {"id": "d9e06dd1-3bf0-44e0-a2f9-405b8124c235", "type": "concept", "title": "bildungsroman"}, {"id": "82b65e74-a9f6-45af-9d55-1ffde6470669", "type": "concept", "title": "family plot"}, {"id": "ad74b0fb-d757-429f-87ee-f13492ee4703", "type": "concept", "title": "omniscient narration"}, {"id": "f6053947-fbf4-43b0-90cf-85eabe50fe72", "type": "concept", "title": "free indirect discourse"}], "description": "The novel becomes a major technology for representing society, interiority, nation, class, and historical change."}, {"id": "8c156202-0035-448c-bee8-30800e782961", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Realism and social systems", "children": [{"id": "6bfc2e4c-9af5-4f97-b02b-89071b327802", "type": "concept", "title": "class mobility"}, {"id": "5bceb4a7-651f-4adc-be34-e9e390422029", "type": "concept", "title": "marriage plot"}, {"id": "0077364d-99a7-4a8e-8423-5ccd4fc91d68", "type": "concept", "title": "property"}, {"id": "b32c8a98-9774-4e9f-8da2-ee649f8badad", "type": "concept", "title": "industrialization"}, {"id": "992c338d-7a2f-4374-b37e-dd315f575670", "type": "concept", "title": "colonial modernity"}, {"id": "361e7c6a-8484-4493-a471-67aded9e0b18", "type": "concept", "title": "urbanization"}], "description": "Students learn how realist form maps money, labor, law, gender, domestic life, empire, and urban space."}, {"id": "b9bb0f90-691a-42be-9c51-087b090db8a7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Beyond Europe", "children": [{"id": "5fe8b420-f052-40fc-9472-c1a91c011069", "type": "concept", "title": "Russian realism"}, {"id": "399a43af-aede-47b8-8df2-8d1b8357fd94", "type": "concept", "title": "Latin American fiction"}, {"id": "bd66430b-dcbc-4357-97af-2c566a89d70d", "type": "concept", "title": "Chinese late Qing fiction"}, {"id": "9998798c-9e4e-4891-bf91-3937db456293", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian novel"}, {"id": "185c6bf0-f589-410f-9072-3b8f0421d818", "type": "concept", "title": "Arabic nahda"}, {"id": "f4bc0462-d976-426f-81cb-3699c0ad82f2", "type": "concept", "title": "African mission-educated writing"}], "description": "The global novel develops through adaptation, translation, local traditions, colonial schools, and anti-colonial imagination."}]}, {"id": "13ba39c4-7cfb-453b-b94f-eb96b2a91466", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernism Globally", "description": "Modernism beyond Europe and the U.S., including little magazines, cities, war, empire, formal rupture, and multilingual experimentation.", "children": [{"id": "f89590ff-dc97-43cb-865b-9210fff731b3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modernist rupture", "children": [{"id": "dfb97028-aeb1-49e1-ba38-85f5a04f1c24", "type": "concept", "title": "fragmentation"}, {"id": "15f23196-e32a-4bc6-9858-1d4aee8befd5", "type": "concept", "title": "stream of consciousness"}, {"id": "95891c51-42ad-43ea-90c6-42ecde1a979d", "type": "concept", "title": "mythic method"}, {"id": "643e776e-f325-483d-a8f6-6ac63abbce24", "type": "concept", "title": "montage"}, {"id": "158e2743-4bb9-4967-8964-f35ac3947332", "type": "concept", "title": "interiority"}, {"id": "f8c01e92-c531-4130-83f1-7efbad3e4767", "type": "concept", "title": "formal difficulty"}], "description": "Modernism is framed as a world-scale response to war, empire, technology, urbanization, and changing consciousness."}, {"id": "6127d539-00fd-4799-a19e-3e837a5e0f02", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Little magazines and networks", "children": [{"id": "97a7528b-a795-4130-b52b-1aeae9793db3", "type": "concept", "title": "little magazine"}, {"id": "c325e324-c013-49fa-ae19-7b57a57afc14", "type": "concept", "title": "manifesto"}, {"id": "a5e77e1a-4b79-47ea-9db9-9d92535be327", "type": "concept", "title": "avant-garde"}, {"id": "41d47c30-1267-4e51-9333-91848de8a788", "type": "concept", "title": "Harlem Renaissance"}, {"id": "4bb16a8f-4a83-4382-9da8-f6b88a413d14", "type": "concept", "title": "Négritude"}, {"id": "981b9400-6c2a-4c77-9492-00b05e56d185", "type": "concept", "title": "Indian modernism"}, {"id": "40140e06-d3f3-4fd9-ab04-02a45d74e722", "type": "concept", "title": "Latin American vanguardia"}], "description": "Students learn how modernism spread through magazines, salons, presses, translation, migration, and colonial education."}, {"id": "972dbb62-a31a-4462-a538-126194236086", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global modernist forms", "children": [{"id": "28aae792-edcc-4082-8e0b-a0c59e09fc3d", "type": "concept", "title": "city writing"}, {"id": "7f93aabf-5485-4938-82d6-749ae76d2cff", "type": "concept", "title": "anti-colonial modernism"}, {"id": "247ec39a-23bb-40fa-87a8-a7af5eef8769", "type": "concept", "title": "vernacular modernism"}, {"id": "f9dbe062-ba27-4136-a3d4-22b858d6a937", "type": "concept", "title": "gender and modernity"}, {"id": "8aa854aa-6d03-4f19-be02-c9c8b6738236", "type": "concept", "title": "diasporic modernism"}, {"id": "4652ce1c-89e3-441e-b44a-ece67ebd7a38", "type": "concept", "title": "world war and trauma"}], "description": "Modernism is not a single European style exported elsewhere; it is remade in many locations."}]}, {"id": "441fecb9-98e4-4c7e-9150-7dea524c2d16", "type": "topic", "title": "Postcolonial Theory, Decolonization, and World Literature", "description": "Reading literature after and against empire through language, nation, subalternity, hybridity, and decolonial critique.", "children": [{"id": "121cd660-dcbd-4b63-aae8-d58d22951847", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empire and decolonization", "children": [{"id": "273c655d-3d22-4b67-acf3-40852f3c6d54", "type": "concept", "title": "colonial discourse"}, {"id": "c953f336-37a9-484e-abe6-92682f9636fc", "type": "concept", "title": "anticolonial nationalism"}, {"id": "41d6a16c-1412-4049-8402-dfa72eabd32a", "type": "concept", "title": "decolonization"}, {"id": "d0ff57b0-46b2-4e13-bd75-ee1c7c60f840", "type": "concept", "title": "neocolonialism"}, {"id": "44595a27-7050-49b7-9068-6ecbb3130665", "type": "concept", "title": "settler colonialism"}, {"id": "0a5b7f08-6097-4f73-865d-3b1db4a83be6", "type": "concept", "title": "imperial afterlives"}], "description": "Postcolonial theory gives students vocabulary for literature after and against empire."}, {"id": "a5b6f5f3-85f1-4f31-bba6-c162578ec34f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Major theoretical conversations", "children": [{"id": "3ce53b3d-1467-41b5-aac5-8eb306fbb8b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Said"}, {"id": "de0d8ac0-281b-42ec-95f4-c9b43d0dfcef", "type": "concept", "title": "Spivak"}, {"id": "30eb4805-1789-4781-9629-e0848c2b48af", "type": "concept", "title": "Bhabha"}, {"id": "6b5fa621-72cf-4454-8f07-7c1d09748210", "type": "concept", "title": "Fanon"}, {"id": "4ec7bf2e-8106-44c6-8722-bb27b069eef4", "type": "concept", "title": "Ngugi"}, {"id": "4427e183-f6c1-4d81-be52-97619790cc9a", "type": "concept", "title": "subaltern studies"}, {"id": "c8c2e11e-8d39-4f88-bd56-dfaf37de28c1", "type": "concept", "title": "hybridity"}, {"id": "c502eb2a-ce7a-4ec9-b713-e17c995767ed", "type": "concept", "title": "mimicry"}], "description": "Students connect literature to the field's major concepts and debates."}, {"id": "c8e90551-d833-4fa8-a836-420bb5252cb8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading postcolonially", "children": [{"id": "c5e5a0d9-3a70-430d-aac7-b2b0aff39d60", "type": "concept", "title": "narrative voice"}, {"id": "6e908f26-04b5-48d1-a4b7-11acfb1649e3", "type": "concept", "title": "allegory"}, {"id": "2f8c1ce0-b9b6-49c9-b5b8-c23d0a2f1ada", "type": "concept", "title": "code-switching"}, {"id": "ef19d6b4-2d46-4026-bfa5-e1b7003d6cf3", "type": "concept", "title": "unreliable narration"}, {"id": "181208d9-3066-454f-a817-864148bc9215", "type": "concept", "title": "archival silence"}, {"id": "43b7286b-5235-4fc7-8442-e7c9dd7aa512", "type": "concept", "title": "nation and gender"}, {"id": "5b55c8ff-57d9-4add-83c8-f9c293d4d290", "type": "concept", "title": "language politics"}], "description": "Theory becomes useful when students connect it to formal choices in poems, novels, drama, memoir, and essays."}]}, {"id": "2095288a-f0d2-4aa0-a7e9-47bf7b8dec3c", "type": "topic", "title": "Diaspora, Migration, Refugee Writing, and Transnational Identity", "description": "Literatures of mobility, exile, migration, border regimes, home, memory, and belonging.", "children": [{"id": "941f03d1-1203-4b49-a1fe-d7be9c60072c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mobility and displacement", "children": [{"id": "96b95b74-97a9-474a-b9d0-93b047fc03e6", "type": "concept", "title": "exile"}, {"id": "a87c6cc3-11ff-4c42-a152-4489eb03fdfc", "type": "concept", "title": "refugeehood"}, {"id": "61d120ce-837a-4217-ad6c-b7a9fa7ecb0e", "type": "concept", "title": "asylum"}, {"id": "4a4f0a1b-ddba-4701-a430-3ceebf0b3ca2", "type": "concept", "title": "labor migration"}, {"id": "66e47ad2-fff3-4786-a960-fff278428f53", "type": "concept", "title": "border regimes"}, {"id": "18dd77f2-3a57-4932-b15a-1a46d93a6eb9", "type": "concept", "title": "statelessness"}, {"id": "432ec8cb-0601-4085-8c9a-9115071fdee0", "type": "concept", "title": "return"}], "description": "Students read migration as both historical condition and literary form."}, {"id": "91b07ba9-9940-4084-9aa1-535d14f6dc04", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diasporic form", "children": [{"id": "da780062-4cef-4c1e-b424-de38cffcd746", "type": "concept", "title": "fragmented chronology"}, {"id": "7e4ecaa8-c9c5-4cfc-b31c-c67aeaff4d1c", "type": "concept", "title": "multi-sited narrative"}, {"id": "0f2658e6-9bf8-4266-bc93-fc50bf659291", "type": "concept", "title": "inherited memory"}, {"id": "01693e04-9549-49d2-ac85-6c68f6edf7d7", "type": "concept", "title": "letters and documents"}, {"id": "fc657868-415e-4fbf-a9df-f5e11e44bee0", "type": "concept", "title": "second generation"}, {"id": "cbf5bf3c-5844-4755-b0a9-ffb3dfea11a4", "type": "concept", "title": "split audience"}], "description": "Diaspora changes narrative time, memory, voice, audience, and genre."}, {"id": "a09e97b7-6a0d-4007-8677-9c379cf9488e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics of representation", "children": [{"id": "e818d6d3-e2d8-4f0d-bd79-eb2722eecf1a", "type": "concept", "title": "witnessing"}, {"id": "d424011f-21b1-4536-b7ab-e2de30204680", "type": "concept", "title": "humanitarian narrative"}, {"id": "9a25b074-948b-4ff7-83f0-aa5db441bbce", "type": "concept", "title": "victimhood and agency"}, {"id": "13e5ee0b-2488-4a72-a740-d39f327ceefa", "type": "concept", "title": "empathy"}, {"id": "807c4ba6-4e24-4d94-8633-a1ec7c2aa72e", "type": "concept", "title": "media framing"}, {"id": "63538387-e43d-4eaa-a438-fc19f25c05c2", "type": "concept", "title": "NGOs"}, {"id": "f64b9279-032e-4353-a370-543d1fc48d5c", "type": "concept", "title": "reader responsibility"}], "description": "Students examine how literature represents suffering, hospitality, agency, and political responsibility."}]}, {"id": "e45aa3e5-21c0-4ffd-bf91-26fb6b956ca2", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Race, Caste, Indigeneity, and Intersectional World Literature", "description": "How identities and power relations reshape canons, genres, embodiment, voice, and reading methods.", "children": [{"id": "c8996a9c-1b57-4655-9ff4-9ce6a26ca1b7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intersectional methods", "children": [{"id": "588aaeb7-4ce3-4e1c-8ced-2ba3cd5dcce5", "type": "concept", "title": "race"}, {"id": "36027204-973e-4383-8be2-cdd8aa4f1ca4", "type": "concept", "title": "gender"}, {"id": "f279c772-7e5b-4d8f-a314-97018922a078", "type": "concept", "title": "sexuality"}, {"id": "fdeeb797-6269-4904-af5a-833007139c8e", "type": "concept", "title": "caste"}, {"id": "fca6bd7e-5ce8-4886-ac6f-60ee32a00322", "type": "concept", "title": "class"}, {"id": "13eb3936-5c77-4618-bdfb-5a91452a4f71", "type": "concept", "title": "disability"}, {"id": "dc4eb129-6867-4afe-bc36-d5e6db57493a", "type": "concept", "title": "religion"}, {"id": "67e4cc57-0c53-4689-92f1-0a80acb83845", "type": "concept", "title": "language"}, {"id": "41496618-2711-4b59-bddd-133ae20a4eb3", "type": "concept", "title": "migration status"}], "description": "Intersectionality prevents single-axis readings of identity and power."}, {"id": "76a685c2-dbf3-4316-b2d1-1572919500a0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Indigeneity and sovereignty", "children": [{"id": "7a0d1eca-1bfc-4519-b9d0-4d6a5caa6261", "type": "concept", "title": "settler colonialism"}, {"id": "ac678c64-31e1-4a86-adbb-b6b4f48474da", "type": "concept", "title": "treaty"}, {"id": "98a65344-1df0-4455-9009-d7f3fc37244f", "type": "concept", "title": "dispossession"}, {"id": "fe485436-e34d-486e-874a-fed32bd850da", "type": "concept", "title": "resurgence"}, {"id": "a62a28a7-aab9-413e-8249-3ebf065643fa", "type": "concept", "title": "oral tradition"}, {"id": "2f4a7f7a-321f-40b6-81fc-855fbec4e9ca", "type": "concept", "title": "language revitalization"}, {"id": "1d8e0224-829f-4087-81e8-01596f559b51", "type": "concept", "title": "protocol"}], "description": "Students read Indigenous and settler-colonial literatures through land, language, sovereignty, and cultural survival."}, {"id": "272aeedc-65c2-4484-8a3d-f423f2d1f571", "type"